HEORY OF THE RATE OF INTEREST","pagenum":"165","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"14","title":"THE CLASSICAL THEORY OF THE RATE OF INTEREST","pagenum":"175","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Appendix on the Rate of Interest in Marshall's Principles of Economics, Ricardo's Principles of Political Economy, and elsewhere","title":"186","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"15","title":"THE PSYCHOLOGICAL AND BUSINESS INCENTIVES TO LIQUIDITY","pagenum":"194","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"16","title":"SUNDRY OBSERVATIONS ON THE NATURE OD CAPITAL","pagenum":"210","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"17","title":"THE ESSENTIAL PROPERTIES OF INTEREST AND MONEY","pagenum":"222","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"18","title":"THE GENERAL THEORY OF EMPLOYMENT","pagenum":"245","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"BOOK V","title":"MONEY-WAGES AND PRICES","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"19","title":"CHANGES IN MONEY-WAGES","pagenum":"257","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Appendix on Prof. Pigou's Theory of Unemployment","title":"272","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"20","title":"THE EMPLOYMENT FUNCTION","pagenum":"280","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"21","title":"THE THEORY OF PRICES","pagenum":"292","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"BOOK VI","title":"SHORT NOTES SUGGESTED BY THE GENERAL THEORY","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"22","title":"NOTES ON THE TRADE CYCLE","pagenum":"313","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"23","title":"NOTES ON MERCANTILISM, THE USURY LAWS, STAMPED MONEY AND THEORIES OF UNDER-CONSUMPTION","pagenum":"333","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"24","title":"CONCLUDING NOTES ON THE SOCIAL PHILOSOPHY TOWARDS WHICH THE GENERAL THEORY MIGHT LEAD","pagenum":"372","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Appendix 1. PRINTING ERRORS IN THE FIRST EDITION","title":"385","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Appendix 2. FLUCTUATIONS IN NET INVESTMENT IN THE UNITED STATES (1936)","title":"386","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"Appendix 3. RELATIVE MOVEMENTS OF REAL WAGES AND OUTPUT (1939)","title":"394","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":1,"label":"INDEX","title":"413","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}},{"level":0,"label":"","title":"The cover-design reproduces a photograph of Keynes and is reproduced by kind permission of Madame Yevonde, 33 Bedford Gardens, London W.8.","pagenum":"","type":{"key":"/type/toc_item"}}],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"John Maynard Keynes was born in Cambridge in 1883, son of John Neville Keynes, later registrary of the university; his mother was one of the earliest women students. Educated at Eton and King's, he passed into the Civil Service in 1906, working for three years in the India Office. He returned to Cambridge as a Fellow of King's in 1909 and remained a Fellow until his death. \r\nSoon after the outbreak of war in 1914 he was called to the Treasury. Over the next four years his ability and his immense capacity for work took him to the top. By 1919 he was principal Treasury representative at the Peace Conference at Versailles. His passionate disagreement with decisions regarding reparations led to his resignation and the writing of The Economic Consequences of the Peace. From then on, Keynes was a national figure, in the centre of every economic argument and the author of countless 'Keynes Plans' to solve one problem after another. \r\nIn 1936 he published the most provocative book written by any economist of his generation. The General Theory, as it is known to all economists, cut through all the Gordian knots of pre-Keynesian discussion of the trade cycle and propounded a new approach to the determination of the level of economic activity, the problems of employment and unemployment, the causes of inflation, the strategies of budgetary policy. Arguments about the book continued until his death in 1946 and still continue today. Despite all that has been written in the subsequent quarter of a century, Keynes and his book still represent the turning-point between the old economics and the new from which each generation of economists needs to take its inspiration and its point of departure towards fresh attempts to carry his work further. \r\nThis new edition provides a complete version of the original text, corrected in the light of subsequent correspondence. It also brings together all of Keynes's prefaces to subsequent foreign translations and prints as appendixes two articles by Keynes, making minor modifications of views set forth in the text."},"covers":[8453230],"physical_format":"Paperback","key":"/books/OL26797706M","publish_places":["London, UK and Basingstoke, UK"],"pagination":"xxxvi","classifications":{},"title":"The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money","number_of_pages":428,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"isbn_10":["0333009428"],"publish_date":"1973","works":[{"key":"/works/OL35951W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"latest_revision":4,"revision":4,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2019-03-20T22:44:20.644198"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2021-08-03T17:16:50.188970"}}},"OLID:OL27305963M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL27305963M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27305963M/The_general_theory_of_employment_interest_and_money","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL27305963M/The_general_theory_of_employment_interest_and_money","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8778962-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["BN Publishing"],"classifications":{},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2019-09-09T04:23:42.905500